
AOMP is a build of the ROCm stack for the compiler development team.
AOMP uses TheRock to build all ROCm components from source.
AOMP is different than building the tip of TheRock in 2 ways.

1) It builds the main branch of TheRock on a specific frozen hash
   key associated with the next release of the AOMP compiler. This
   TheRock hash key implies a set of hash keys for each of TheRock
   submodules with the exception of submodule amd-llvm and hipify.
2) The submodules amd-llvm and hipify use the latest amd-staging
   branch which is the integration branch for compiler development.

This directory (aomp/tr_aomp) contains all the support to build AOMP
using TheRock.

The current build process is:

1) ./tr_clone_aomp.sh
   
2) ./tr_build_aomp.sh
   check TheRock/build/build.out

The default for the env variable TR_AOMP_REPOS is /work/$USER/git/tr-aomp.
tr_clone_aomp.sh will clone the aomp repo to $TR_AOMP_REPOS/aomp
and pull most recent updates.
Unless TheRock repo already exists, tr_clone_aomp.sh will then clone
TheRock repo into $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock and initialize it.

It then checks out amd-staging branch of submodules amd-llvm
and hipify and pulls recent updates to just those two submodules.
It is always safe to run tr_clone_aomp.sh. Typically, all that
happens is an update to amd-staging branch of llvm-project and hipify.

It is rare that no info file for the current release exists.
This occurs when the release manager starts a new AOMP release.
Most developers will use the info file provided by the release manager
at the beginning of an AOMP release.
If no info file is present, the build process will initialize a new
AOMP release by creating the info file for the release with the current
tip of TheRock repo. Subsequent builds will remain on the shakey
found when the release is initialized.  Creating new AOMP releases will
be how the AOMP team keeps pace with TheRock while insulating developers
from other ROCm development churn. Of course, AOMP developers are always
exposed to the churn in the llvm-project compiler repository.

Any changes or fixes to TheRock repo or its submodules during the development
of the AOMP release, can be shared with the team by creating a set of patches
with the script tr_create_patch.sh.

tr_build_aomp.sh builds ROCm into $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock/build/dist/rocm.
using the standard TheRock build process with cmake and ninja.
Then copies using rsync from $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock/build/dist/rocm 
to $AOMP_INSTALL_DIR.

These two symlinks help to iterate development of llvm-project. 
ln -sf $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock/compiler/amd-llvm $TR_AOMP_REPOS/llvm-project
ln -sf $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock/build/compiler/amd-llvm/build $TR_AOMP_REPOS/llvm-project/build

Compiler development flow: edit, build, install, test
EDIT
  - cd git/tr-aomp/llvm-project ; edit files in the llvm source tree

UPDATE BUILD
  - cd $TR_AOMP_REPOS/llvm-project/build
    run ./build_install_aomp_from_therock.sh which does these two steps:
      ninja therock-dist
      rsync -av $TR_AOMP_REPOS/TheRock/build/dist/rocm/ $AOMP_INSTALL_DIR/
TEST
  - test with AOMP=$AOMP_INSTALL_DIR or
    AOMP=/work/$USER/rocm/aomp which is a symlink to $AOMP_INSTALL_DIR

